97:
The mayor and council may appoint and hire a public safety director.
(1) In the event that a public safety director has been appointed, then the public safety director shall:
a. Coordinate with, consult with, and assist the chief of police in the matters set forth in section 24-22(1) and (2);
b. Serve as a liaison between the police department and both the police committee and city council for any issues, matters, and policies governed by the police committee;
c. Serve as a liaison between the fire department and both the fire committee and city council for any issues, matters, and policies governed by the fire committee;
d. Coordinate police department, fire department, or any other public safety matters with the emergency management coordinators for the city and the county;
e. Report to the city administrator for all matters concerning the police department, fire department, and other public safety issues; provided, however, that the city administrator shall not be prohibited from working directly with the police and fire chiefs in any matters for which the city administrator has been given responsibility by the mayor and council;
(2) If the position of city administrator is vacant, the public safety director shall serve as supervisor of the chief of police and fire chief, and report to the police and fire committees, and to the mayor and city council on any matters concerning the police or fire departments.
(Code 2005, § 3-1-4; Ord. of 10-8-2012)

118:
The hospital authority of the city shall be deemed to exercise public and essential governmental function and shall have all of the powers necessary or convenient to carry out and effectuate the purposes and provisions of said act, including, but without limiting the generality of the foregoing, the following powers:
(1) To sue and to be sued;
(2) To plead and to be impleaded;
(3) To have a seal and to alter the same;
(4) To acquire, own, hold, lease and dispose of real property;
(5) To make and execute contracts and other instruments necessary to exercise the powers of the authority;
(6) To acquire, leave and operate hospital projects and to provide, construct, reconstruct, make improvements, alterations and repairs of such project, and to lease and rent for any number of years any land, buildings, structures or facilities in any existing, or hereafter established hospital project, and to establish rates and charges for the use and of the services of the authority;
(7) To accept gifts, grants, or devices of any property;
(8) To acquire by the right of eminent domain any property essential to the purpose of the authority, to sell, lease, exchange, transfer, assign, pledge, mortgage or dispose of any real or personal property or interest therein;
(9) To mortgage, pledge, or assign any revenues, including tolls, charges, or fees received by the authority to issue certificates of indebtedness for the purpose of providing funds for carrying out the duties of the authority, provided such certificates shall not extend beyond a period of 40 years from the date of issuance thereof and provided further that such certificates shall be repayable from the revenues realized from the project or any part thereof;
(10) To borrow money for any corporate purposes;
(11) To appoint officers, agents and employees;
(12) To make use of any facilities afforded by the federal government, the state, and the county, or any agencies or instrumentalities thereof, and to exercise any and all powers now or hereafter possessed by private corporations performing similar functions, in general, including the right to adopt bylaws and establish operating procedures, and to have the power and perform all functions as authorized and provided under said act of 1964, in particular as codified in O.C.G.A. §§ 31-7-70 through 31-7-96.
(Code 1974, § 7-202; Code 2005, § 2-6-2; Ord. of 3-13-1984; Ord. of 8-11-2008)

146:
(a) The purposes of this chapter shall include, without necessarily being limited to, the following:
(1) Compliance with and effectuation of the state law regarding alcoholic beverages;
(2) Promoting the health, safety and general welfare of the community;
(3) Ensuring that those who sell alcoholic beverages are properly licensed;
